Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
if you're watching this you probably  have seen at least one video by business  casual  the channel is a great example of what's  possible in terms of business  entertainment content on youtube  and is definitely an influence on some  of my videos but the channel went silent  about a year ago  and i wanted to find out why so i dug  into it even if you've never heard of  the channel before  i think you'll want to stick around and  watch this video it's a really  interesting story that highlights a  darker side of entrepreneurship that we  don't often hear about  usually when youtube channels die they  do it slowly  the content gets boring and repetitive  and view numbers start decreasing but  that's not the case for business casual  the last video on the channel got nearly  2 million views  and at 40 minutes long it was clearly a  ton of work to put together  so why would a successful channel like  business casual just throw in the towel  all of a sudden  the answer was not what i was expecting  at all  and contains a really important lesson  for entrepreneurs working on any big  project  not just youtube creators long story  short the folks behind business casual  are suing youtube  which is pretty weird because it seems  like youtube has been quite good to the  channel in general  business casual has nearly a million  subscribers and none of their content  seems to be controversial or demonetized  but if you scroll through the business  casual twitter feed  you will see that whoever is running the  account now is on a crusade against  youtube  in fact the twitter account has posted  basically the same tweet  every day for the last hundred days  straight so i had to figure out what was  driving this  as a little bit of backstory business  casual was started by this guy  jordan kuzminov he lives in bulgaria and  started the channel back in 2016 when he  was studying  business at the rotterdam school of  management i went back through some of  his old reddit posts and he seems like a  really genuine guy  he's just super interested in  entrepreneurial stories the channel grew  significantly over the past few years  and he was even able to bring on a  narrator and animators to help make the  videos  the channel currently has about 80  million views which means that it could  easily have generated a million dollars  in revenue  across youtube ads and sponsorship deals  but looking at jordan's linkedin profile  it seems like he ended his relationship  with the channel in september of 2019  so i started looking into what happened  around that time i'm not a hundred  percent sure  but it looks like he either sold part or  all of the channel  to a guy named alex edson who runs a  company in scottsdale arizona  named ebrands this guy alex has started  a few different businesses  and has been involved with a bunch of  youtube channels over the past few years  and it's alex's name on the youtube  lawsuit that business casual is filing  so it looks like alex is really the one  driving everything now  and constantly posting to twitter  interestingly the business casual  twitter account actually posted the full  243-page lawsuit  so i read through it to get an idea of  what's really going on here basically  the lawsuit comes down to some pretty  standard copyright infringement claims  essentially it looks like another  youtube channel took clips from business  casual videos  and used them in new videos without  permission from the business casual team  for obvious reasons  i'm not going to show any of the  copyrighted footage but if you want to  check it out  i'll link to the clips in the  description below the actual offending  clips are fairly short  one is just seven seconds long and it's  just a slow  zoom on a public domain image it's clear  that the video was stolen  but it's not like they re-uploaded a  whole video or anything like that  youtube actually did take down the  videos that contained the offending  clips  but alex edson doesn't seem to think  that's enough and this is where it gets  really interesting  see the channels that used the  copyrighted footage from business casual  were affiliated with rt which is a  russian state-controlled network  rt used to be called russia today and  has been accused of spreading  disinformation and conspiracy theories  in fact in 2017 rt america was ordered  to register as a foreign agent with the  united states department of justice  so when i first saw the tweets from  business casual in the lawsuit  i thought that maybe this was a personal  vendetta jordan the original creator of  the channel  is from bulgaria after all and  bulgaria's relationship with russia has  been deteriorating over the past decade  i assumed that jordan had taken the  copyright infringement as an opportunity  to punish russia  and maybe he had some personal family  history with russia that motivated him  to stop making new videos  just to focus on this lawsuit but now i  think it's more complicated  jordan isn't listed on any of the  lawsuit filings and it doesn't look like  he's affiliated with the channel in a  meaningful way anymore  alex edson on the other hand is on every  email thread with rt  and youtube representatives and seems to  be the sole driving force behind the  lawsuit so why does this entrepreneur  from arizona want to get russia today  kicked off of youtube so badly  well there are a few factors here first  is the obvious political angle  russia has been a major american rival  for the past hundred years  and tensions between russia and america  were particularly high during the cold  war  more recently russian interference in  the 2016 united states election  became an extremely hot topic donald  trump was a deeply unpopular president  throughout his time in office  and many of his detractors hold russia  accountable for his election more  recently though  the narrative has flipped a bit with  trump kicked off nearly  every social media platform  conservatives have been asking the  question  why is the president of the united  states banned from social media  but russia's state-run accounts aren't  so it's possible that alex edson has a  strong stance here  and wants to get russia kicked off of  youtube for political reasons  but i actually think there's another  layer to this story  i'm not a legal expert but the business  casual lawsuit looks weak to me we're  talking about a few short clips used for  just a few seconds on channels that  publish  literally dozens of videos per day  youtube should definitely take them down  and business casual should receive the  advertising revenue from those videos  since they produced the original content  but it looks like the footage was used  in the context of a much larger video  and the rt channels weren't directly  re-uploading whole videos from business  casual  in order to steal ad revenue if you  consider the actual economic damage done  to the business casual channel here  it looks pretty minimal these clips were  used on arabic channels  and business casual doesn't even post  content in arabic so there isn't really  a good argument that people would watch  the stolen videos  instead of business casual's original  content thus depriving them of views and  ad revenue but i think alex is playing a  bigger economic game with this lawsuit  see youtube and really all the big tech  companies are under immense pressure  right now from the united states  government  as i've mentioned in previous videos  both elizabeth warren on the left  and josh hawley on the right are calling  to break up big tech companies  the last thing youtube needs right now  is a lawsuit gaining steam that makes it  look like they are helping russia spread  disinformation  so this case could easily get settled  out of court  essentially youtube might just pay alex  to go away  that could be a great outcome for him  even though business casual brings in  decent viewer numbers and probably makes  thousands of dollars every month  the content costs a lot to produce and  getting a big payday from youtube  could be significantly more profitable  regardless of what happens with the  lawsuit  it looks like business casual isn't  working on any new content at the moment  so if you're looking to hear interesting  stories about successful companies  you should subscribe to my channel i  just put out a great story about how  discord grew into a multi-billion dollar  company  and next week i'll be releasing the full  story of epic games who created fortnite  in every video i try to not only tell  the story of the company in an engaging  way  but also pull out some key takeaways  that any entrepreneur could apply to  their own business  and there's a big takeaway here about  the business casual story  getting copied happens all the time in  business sometimes it's a direct copy  and you can take legal action but  sometimes it's just a competitive  business and you can't do anything about  it regardless  you can't lose sight of what you're  building the business casual fan base  just wants to watch engaging  documentaries about business  and all this focus on suing youtube  doesn't help them at all when i started  my first company soylant  someone in russia funnily enough  actually put up a copycat website  designed to steal customer credit card  information people thought that they  were buying from us  but they were actually just sending  money directly to these russian hackers  it was annoying but we didn't let it  distract us from our mission of making a  great product if someone copies you  it might be extremely frustrating but  you can't let it consume you  you need to stay focused on your  business there is another option for  business casual though one that would  let them put pressure on russian media  while still making great content they  could start making videos about this  whole story the most successful channels  on youtube  are all personality driven and i know  that i would definitely  watch behind the scenes content of alex  edson's fight against russian media that  would be extremely interesting content  and i think a lot of the business casual  fan base would follow along regardless  of what you think about russia  or where you stand on copyright  infringement i think we can all agree  it's unfortunate when a channel that  produces great content just stops  in the meantime please consider watching  some of the videos on my channel  the youtube algorithm thinks you'll like  this one in particular

